#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186233757 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7614a05-f221-5fda-b491-6893f44a1a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with Multiple Common Factors"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3a8407af-a47a-5ef1-a65f-b4b72289472c" :question/math-academy-id "q-29966" :question/problem "If $\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}}\\cdot X=\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c},$ then what is $X?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}}\\cdot X=\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c},$ we have\n\n$$\nX=\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c}÷\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c}÷\\frac{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}}{25{c}^{3}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12a{b}^{3}}{15c}\\cdot \\frac{25{c}^{3}}{24{a}^{3}{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{12\\cdot a{b}^{2}\\cdot b}{3\\cdot 5c}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot 5c\\cdot {c}^{2}}{12\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot a{b}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{12}\\cdot \\cancel{a{b}^{2}}\\cdot b}{3\\cdot \\cancel{5c}}\\cdot \\frac{5\\cdot \\cancel{5c}\\cdot {c}^{2}}{\\cancel{12}\\cdot 2{a}^{2}\\cdot \\cancel{a{b}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{b}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5{c}^{2}}{2{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\frac{5b{c}^{2}}{6{a}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297256 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068760 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7614a05-f221-5fda-b491-6893f44a1a5f" :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Rational Expressions with Multiple Common Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3c4f1d16-78b3-5045-8bb5-5579fec4543a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295613} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3602d9-f3c5-5344-8c7b-288df52749cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{y}^{2}}{k+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295709} {:answer/id #uuid "93b20eed-4388-595a-84d8-977910934218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{k+4}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296058} {:answer/id #uuid "aa7ed919-742c-5a4c-b988-a4265e3f6f06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{y}^{2}}{44(k+2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296162} {:answer/id #uuid "e8492be0-a6b9-5d09-afa1-2e0f3bbeb2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296502}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8492be0-a6b9-5d09-afa1-2e0f3bbeb2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296502} :answer-field/id #uuid "17d9d487-4676-539c-9b1c-0c3a024bdb73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "964f86e1-4f13-55f4-a1ab-8caf3da2db2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-29970" :question/problem "If $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ then what is $Y?$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}\\cdot Y=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}},$ we have\n\n$$\nY=\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y}.\n$$\n\nTo compute this quotient, we just need to multiply by the reciprocal of the divisor:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}÷\\frac{2(k+2)}{121y} & = \\\\ \\frac{4(k+2)^{2}}{11{y}^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{121y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot 2(k+2)\\cdot (k+2)}{11y\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot 11y}{2(k+2)} & = \\\\ \\frac{2\\cdot \\cancel{2(k+2)}\\cdot (k+2)}{\\cancel{11y}\\cdot {y}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{11\\cdot \\cancel{11y}}{\\cancel{2(k+2)}} & = \\\\ \\frac{2(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\cdot 11 & = \\\\ \\frac{22(k+2)}{{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
